WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.360
Thought you knew all there was to know about
Avengers: Endgame?

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:03.880
Think again.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.680
A few eagle-eyed Marvel aficionados have spotted
an intriguing Easter egg tucked in the super-sized

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.460
superhero film.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.960
Taking to the site's r/MovieDetails forum,
one Reddit user uploaded a screenshot of an

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:18.230
Avengers: Endgame scene that shows what seems
to be a travel log of all the time periods

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:19.980
the Avengers jumped in to in the film.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.780
The log appears when the 2014 version of Nebula
is executing her plan to activate the time-travel

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:29.060
machine at Avengers HQ to bring Thanos to
the present day in 2023.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.260
In the top right section of the screen, one
can spot the supposed user history, which

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.300
details in part that Earth's Mightiest Heroes
first traveled to May 2012, aka the setting

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.660
of the first Avengers movie.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.330
Viewers saw this play out during the time
heist mission in Avengers: Endgame, but what

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:48.140
they didn't see is some of the heroes apparently
traveling to January 2988, a date which is

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.280
mysteriously included as the next entry in
the time-travel log.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.520
Even weirder, it looks like the date specifically
says "2988 BC."

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.760
But that's not all.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.720
A mention of the year 2988 can also be seen
in a different sequence in Avengers: Endgame.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.660
When Chris Hemsworth's Thor is recapping the
events of Thor: The Dark World and describing

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.259
the nature of the Aether, aka the Reality
Stone, he shares a story about his grandfather

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.660
King Bor, an Asgardian who seized the Aether
from the Dark Elves thousands of years ago.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:21.000
"My grandfather, many years ago, had to hide
the stone from the Dark Elves… oOoOoOh…

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.280
scary beings…”

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.420
Behind Thor, one of the screens reads "2988
B.C.", which may well be the same year that

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.940
Bor battled against the Dark Elf forces and
Malekith the Accursed to obtain the Aether.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:38.980
"Millennia ago, the most ruthless of their
kind, Malekith, sought to transform our universe

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:41.619
back into one of eternal night."

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:46.320
In the version of Avengers: Endgame we all
saw, Thor and Rocket travel to Asgard in 2013,

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.560
intersecting with the events of Thor: The
Dark World.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.260
They then proceed to obtain the Aether by
extracting it from Jane Foster.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.520
However, the screen displaying the year 2988
B.C. hints that Thor and Rocket may have at

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.509
least discussed traveling back to the First
Battle of Svartalfheim instead, snatching

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:05.120
up the Aether way, way back in the past, instead
of just 2013.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.859
There are some reasons why this might have
been an option for the team.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.180
Thor clearly had mixed feelings about returning
to Asgard in the state we see him in in Endgame

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:16.500
depressed, anxious, overweight, and beating
himself up over his perceived mistakes he

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.060
made during the battle with Thanos.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.780
Plus, he knew it would be painful to see his
homeland in the moments before thousands of

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:22.660
people were killed.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.350
Not that jumping back to the bloody First
Battle of Svartalfheim would be any less intense

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.180
for Thor, but there would be at least a little
bit of emotional distance given how long ago

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.120
the battle took place.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.591
It seems pretty plausible that some potential
cut or draft of Avengers: Endgame saw the

00:02:35.591 --> 00:02:40.450
heroes leaping back to the year 2988 B.C.,
before it was decided to loop in the more

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.720
modern-day events of Thor: The Dark World
instead.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.860
As far as we can tell, the filmmakers haven't
talked about this, so we really don't know.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.040
"I have no idea, we're talking about time
travel here."

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.700
Some people think the out-of-place date has
nothing to do with the plot of the movie,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.660
and is instead a reference to an alternate
Earth from Marvel's "What If" Comics: Earth-2988.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.720
In that reality, Wolverine became a horseman
of the powerful villain Apocalypse.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.700
Some see the number as possibly being a clue
for how the X-Men will join the MCU, but that

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:06.920
seems like a stretch.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.010
But it's just as likely that some version
of the movie saw Thor and Rocket taking an

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.290
alternate route to get the Aether, and not
all of the computer graphics in the final

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.800
movie got the message but that's just speculation.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.420
We may never unravel the full mystery of this
strange time travel detail.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.180
Either way, it goes to show that there's a
lot more going on in this three-hour movie

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.740
then anyone at first realized.

